@page "/computed-background-color"

<p>
    Each stage below feeds its inner element to <code>ComputedBackgroundColor.resolve(...)</code>.
    The resolved color drives the left-edge overflow gradient.
</p>

<section class="demo">
    <h3>1 Layer</h3>
    <p>A single translucent layer blended onto the page background.</p>

    <div class="layer layer-1">
                    <div @ref="_innerElements[0]" class="inner-layer">
                    Testing
                </div>
            </div>
        </section>

<section class="demo">
    <h3>2 Layers</h3>
    <p>Two translucent layers stacked, blended top-to-bottom onto the page background.</p>

    <div class="layer layer-1">
        <div class="layer layer-2">
            <div @ref="_innerElements[1]" class="inner-layer">
                Testing
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="demo">
    <h3>3 Layers</h3>
    <p>Three translucent layers stacked, blended top-to-bottom onto the page background.</p>

    <div class="layer layer-1">
        <div class="layer layer-2">
            <div class="layer layer-3">
                <div @ref="_innerElements[2]" class="inner-layer">
                    Testing
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="demo">
    <h3>Opaque layer in the middle</h3>
    <p>
        An opaque layer hides everything behind it. The outer translucent red layer must be ignored;
        only the blue layer above the opaque green one contributes to the result.
    </p>

    <div class="layer layer-1">
        <div class="layer layer-opaque">
            <div @ref="_innerElements[3]" class="inner-layer">
                Testing
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="demo">
    <h3>Fully transparent layer</h3>
    <p>
        A <code>transparent</code> layer paints nothing and must be skipped, so the result equals the
        single translucent red layer below it.
    </p>

    <div class="layer layer-1">
        <div class="layer layer-clear">
            <div @ref="_innerElements[4]" class="inner-layer">
                Testing
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="demo">
    <h3>Opacity multiplied with alpha</h3>
    <p>
        The layer uses <code>background-color: rgba(255, 0, 0, 0.5)</code> together with
        <code>opacity: 0.5</code>, so its effective alpha is <code>0.25</code>.
    </p>

    <div class="layer layer-opacity">
        <div @ref="_innerElements[5]" class="inner-layer">
            Testing
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="demo">
    <h3>Non-uniform background</h3>
    <p>
        A translucent blue layer covers the whole area while a translucent red layer covers only the
        left half. The inner element is 50% wide and left-aligned, so every probe point lands on the
        red-over-blue overlap and resolves to a single blended color.
    </p>

    <div class="split split-start">
        <div class="split-full split-blue"></div>
        <div class="split-left-half split-red"></div>
        <div @ref="_innerElements[6]" class="inner-layer split-inner split-inner-half">
            Testing
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="demo">
    <h3>Split background with a half-width probe</h3>
    <p>
        The area is split into a translucent red left half and a translucent blue right half. The inner
        element is 50% wide and left-aligned, so it only overlaps the red half and the overflow gradient
        turns red.
    </p>

    <div class="split split-start">
        <div class="split-half split-left"></div>
        <div class="split-half split-right"></div>
        <div @ref="_innerElements[7]" class="inner-layer split-inner split-inner-half">
            Testing
        </div>
    </div>
</section>
